  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 216 AM CST Fri Nov 6 2026\n\nConditions early this morning are relatively quiet as the low \npressure that brought widespread rain earlier this week has departed \nwell to the east and a ridge begins to build. Overcast skies are \nlingering early this morning over much of northwest WI, as north-\nnortheast winds and cold temps aloft produce lake effect clouds. \nLight lake effect snow has also been reported early this morning \naround the snowbelts of the Penokee Range as 850mb temps have \nreduced to around -7 degC and lake sfc temps remain around 9 degC. \n\nThe resulting delta-T of 16 degrees has been able to produce around \n200 J/kg of lake induced CAPE per the HRRR at KIWD. Additional \naccumulations are expected to be minimal at 0.2\" or less, mostly \noccurring on elevated and grassy surfaces. Expect lake effect snow \nto end later this morning as drier air arrives, with snow \naccumulations quickly melting today as temps warm into the low to \nmid 40s.  \n\nIn sharp contrast to the wet weather that was seen earlier this \nweek, a much drier pattern is expected to settle over the CWA \nstarting today and persist through much of next week. Global models \nlargely keep the storm track north of the International Border, \nalthough several shortwaves will potentially move through the CWA on \nSaturday into early Sunday and on Tuesday into Wednesday. \nPrecipitation chances are minimal to none in the CWA as these \nshortwaves propagate through, as the vast majority of moisture \nassociated with these troughs is anticipated to remain in \nManitoba and Ontario. \n\nHigh temperatures today through the remainder of the forecast period \nare expected to be above-normal. For context, normal high temps for \nthis time of year are around the upper 30s to low 40s with overnight \nlows in the 20s. This above-normal trend is very likely to continue \nthrough mid-November as the Climate Prediction Center has the \nNorthland under an area likely to see above-normal temps.",
